    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\nI&#39;ve been playing around a good bit with the simsem package, and rather like it.  One thing I&#39;m curious about is using it for simulations with known values for exogenous variables.  I haven&#39;t quite found a way to get the output I&#39;m looking for yet, and was\r\n wondering if anyone on this list had done something along these lines with simsem.\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>So, a short example.  Let&#39;s say I have the following:</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>library(lavaan)</div>\r\n<div>library(simsem)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>set.seed(20130322)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>adf &lt;- data.frame(expand.grid(x1=1:100, x2=1:100))</div>\r\n<div>adf &lt;- within(adf, {</div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre\"></span>y1 &lt;- rnorm(100, x1*2, 20) </div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre\"></span>y2 &lt;- rnorm(100, x2 + y1, 30)</div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre\"></span>})</div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre\"></span></div>\r\n<div>pairs(adf, gap=0)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>model &lt;- &#39;</div>\r\n<div> y1 ~ x1</div>\r\n<div> y2 ~ y1 + x2&#39;</div>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n<div>fit &lt;- sem(model, adf)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Now, let&#39;s say I wanted to know</div>\r\n<div> a) the value of y2 and y1 if x1 and x2 were both 2 and</div>\r\n<div> b) the error around that from simulations.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>I am unclear how one uses generate to determine those two items.  I&#39;ve tried a couple of different things with realData, but it seems that is to generate a matching covariance structure.  Is there a way to plugin these values of x1 and x2 in, say, a new\r\n data frame, and get both the prediction and bootstrapped error around these predictions using the simsem package?</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Thanks, and sorry if I missed something in the documentation.  I just couldn&#39;t quite find what I was looking for, and I&#39;m sure the answer is simple...</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>-Jarrett</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<div><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-indent:0px;text-transform: