   "subject": "Re: [simsem] generating predictions for fit models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div>The simsem package needs the matrix format to generate data with covariates. The example is in the following link:<br><br><a href=\"https://github.com/simsem/simsem/blob/master/SupportingDocs/Examples/Version05/exMultipleFormat/covData.R\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/simsem/simsem/blob/master/SupportingDocs/Examples/Version05/exMultipleFormat/covData.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792433102478000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2VkcQaqVLxqZvEO5F58YwZ\">https://github.com/simsem/simsem/blob/master/SupportingDocs/Examples/Version05/exMultipleFormat/covData.R</a> <br>\r\n<br></div>However, we can get around the matrix specification by a little trick. I will show an example here:<br><br>library(simsem)<br>library(lavaan)<br><br></div># Create MIMIC model with one factor<br><div>script &lt;- &quot;<br>\r\nf1 =~ 1*y1 + 1?y2 + 1?y3<br>f1 ~~ 0.75?f1<br>f1 ~ 0.5?x1<br>f1 ~ 0.5?x2<br>y1 ~~ 0.5?y1<br>y2 ~~ 0.5?y2<br>y3 ~~ 0.5?y3<br>&quot;<br><br></div><div># Use the cfa function to read the script without analyzing any data<br></div>\r\n<div>template &lt;- cfa(script, do.fit=FALSE, fixed.x=TRUE)<br><br></div><div># Use model.lavaan function to transcribe the output of lavaan to simsem matrix format<br></div><div>template2 &lt;- model.lavaan(template)<br>\r\n<br></div><div># Create covariate data. The number of rows must equal to the target sample size.<br></div><div>covData &lt;- data.frame(x1 = rbinom(100, 1, 0.5), x2 = rpois(100, 4))<br><br></div><div># To generate data with covariates, the covData argument is used to supply the exogenous covariates<br>\r\n</div><div>dat &lt;- generate(template2, n = 100, covData=covData)<br><br></div><div># Try to analyze a single data set<br></div><div>out &lt;- cfa(script, data = dat, fixed.x=TRUE)<br>summary(out)<br><br></div><div># Run a simulation<br>\r\n</div><div>simout &lt;- sim(nRep = 20, n = 100, model = template2, covData=covData)<br>summary(simout)<br><br><div>I am not sure I answer all of your questions. Hopefully, this script will help you start your project. A good documentation on the exogenous covariates has not written yet. I will try to write it soon.<br>\r\n<br>Sunthud<br></div><div><br><br></div></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Mon, Mar 25, 2013 at 9:34 AM, Jarrett Byrnes <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">Jarrett...@umb.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\nI&#39;ve been playing around a good bit with the simsem package, and rather like it.  One thing I&#39;m curious about is using it for simulations with known values for exogenous variables.  I haven&#39;t quite found a way to get the output I&#39;m looking for yet, and was\r\n wondering if anyone on this list had done something along these lines with simsem.\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>So, a short example.  Let&#39;s say I have the following:</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>library(lavaan)</div>\r\n<div>library(simsem)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>set.seed(20130322)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>adf &lt;- data.frame(expand.grid(x1=1:100, x2=1:100))</div>\r\n<div>adf &lt;- within(adf, {</div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\"></span>y1 &lt;- rnorm(100, x1*2, 20) </div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\"></span>y2 &lt;- rnorm(100, x2 + y1, 30)</div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\"></span>})</div>\r\n<div><span style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\"></span></div>\r\n<div>pairs(adf, gap=0)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>model &lt;- &#39;</div>\r\n<div> y1 ~ x1</div>\r\n<div> y2 ~ y1 + x2&#39;</div>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n<div>fit &lt;- sem(model, adf)</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Now, let&#39;s say I wanted to know</div>\r\n<div> a) the value of y2 and y1 if x1 and x2 were both 2 and</div>\r\n<div> b) the error around that from simulations.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>I am unclear how one uses generate to determine those two items.  I&#39;ve tried a couple of different things with realData, but it seems that is to generate a matching covariance structure.  Is there a way to plugin these values of x1 and x2 in, say, a new\r\n data frame, and get both the prediction and bootstrapped error around these predictions using the simsem package?</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Thanks, and sorry if I missed something in the documentation.  I just couldn&#39;t quite find what I was looking for, and I&#39;m sure the answer is simple...</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>-Jarrett</div>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<div><span style=\"text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-variant:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;line-height:normal;border-collapse:separate;text-transform:none;font-size:medium;white-space:normal;font-family:Helvetica;word-spacing:0px\"><span style=\"text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-variant:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;line-height:normal;border-collapse:separate;text-transform:none;font-size:medium;white-space:normal;font-family:Helvetica;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-variant:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;line-height:normal;border-collapse:separate;text-transform:none;font-size:medium;white-space:normal;font-family:Helvetica;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-variant:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;line-height:normal;border-collapse:separate;text-transform:none;font-size:medium;white-space:normal;font-family:Helvetica;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-variant:normal;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;line-height:normal;border-collapse:separate;text-transform:none;font-size:medium;white-space:normal;font-family:Helvetica;word-spacing:0px\">\r\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n<span style=\"text-indent:0px;letter-spacing:normal;font-variant:normal;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;lin